---
/**
 * QuoteForm — the written enquiry SPEC section 1 says is this site's whole job.
 *
 * A NATIVE `<form action method="POST">`, POSTING STRAIGHT TO FORMSPREE. That
 * is the baseline and it is what runs when the script at the foot of this file
 * does not. No `@formspree/ajax`, no `@formspree/react`, no validation script:
 * both Formspree client libraries are dependencies, the list in ARCHITECTURE
 * section 1 is closed, and the AJAX submit below costs 23 lines without
 * either of them.
 *
 * THE ONE PIECE OF CLIENT JAVASCRIPT ON THIS SITE LIVES AT THE FOOT OF THIS
 * FILE, AND IT IS A DELIBERATE, NAMED EXCEPTION — ARCHITECTURE section 2 and
 * CLAUDE.md both name it. Do not read it as a violation of the zero-JS rule and
 * delete it. Why it exists: Formspree's `_next` redirect is a PAID feature, so
 * on the free tier a visitor who submits lands on Formspree's own English,
 * Formspree-branded thanks page — the last screen a customer sees at the moment
 * they have committed — while `/aitah` and `/en/thank-you` sit built and
 * correct. Formspree DOES accept AJAX on the free tier; only the server-side
 * redirect is gated. So the script POSTs with `fetch` and navigates to our own
 * page itself.
 *
 * IT IS PROGRESSIVE ENHANCEMENT, NOT A DEPENDENCY. With JavaScript disabled
 * there is no listener, nothing calls `preventDefault()`, and the browser
 * performs exactly the native POST it performed before the script existed —
 * landing on Formspree's page, which is what happens today. The enquiry arrives
 * either way. Degrading to TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR rather than to broken is the whole
 * basis on which the exception was taken.
 *
 * VALIDATION IS THE BROWSER'S. `required`, `type="email"`, `type="tel"`,
 * `inputmode`, `autocomplete`. No custom messages, no scripted checks, no
 * `novalidate`. A field that cannot be expressed in HTML is not enforced — see
 * the note on "phone OR email" in `i18n/contact.ts`.
 *
 * REQUIRED: name, email, address. OPTIONAL: phone, area, message. The reasoning
 * is in `i18n/contact.ts` and it is deliberate, not an omission.
 *
 * THE SERVICE OPTIONS COME FROM THE COLLECTION, never from a list typed here,
 * so they cannot drift from the five service pages. `servicesFor()` runs the
 * same locale guard and cross-locale assertion the nav, the grid and the price
 * table run.
 *
 * SPAM IS `_gotcha` AND NOTHING ELSE. No CAPTCHA, no third-party script, no
 * challenge. **The script below does not reopen this.** Its argument was never
 * "we ship no JavaScript" — it is that a challenge is a third-party dependency
 * and a third-party request, and that it BREAKS THE NO-JS BASELINE: a visitor
 * without JavaScript can complete this form today and could not complete a
 * CAPTCHA. The honeypot survives with the script exactly as it did without it,
 * because `new FormData(form)` carries every named control — `_gotcha`
 * included, confirmed on the wire rather than assumed.
 *
 * The honeypot is hidden from people AND from screen readers: off-screen alone
 * would still be tabbable and announced, so it also carries `tabindex="-1"` and
 * `aria-hidden="true"`.
 */
import { formspreeAction } from '../config/formspree'
import { site } from '../config/site'
import { contactContent } from '../i18n/contact'
import { servicesFor } from '../i18n/services'
import { path } from '../i18n/utils'
import type { Locale } from '../i18n/ui'

interface Props {
  locale: Locale
  /** Preselects a service. The select is never empty — there is no blank option. */
  defaultService?: string
}

const { locale, defaultService } = Astro.props
const c = contactContent(locale)
const f = c.form
const services = await servicesFor(locale)

/**
 * Formspree needs an ABSOLUTE url in `_next` — a relative path is rejected.
 * Built from `site.domain` and the route map, never typed.
 *
 * `_next` IS STILL IGNORED BY FORMSPREE, AND THE REASON IS THE PLAN, NOT THIS
 * CODE. Measured in Phase 6: two identical POSTs were both accepted (302) and
 * both redirected to `https://formspree.io/thanks` rather than here. The custom
 * "Thank You" redirect is a paid feature — Formspree's own docs list it as
 * "Available on: Personal, Professional, Business plans" — and this account is
 * on the free tier. Everything else works: the submission arrives, all seven
 * fields map correctly, `_subject` is honoured.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGED IS THAT THE PAGE NOW READS THIS FIELD AND NAVIGATES ITSELF. The
 * script at the foot of this file takes the value out of the rendered `_next`
 * input rather than from a second copy of `nextUrl`, which is what keeps this
 * one expression the single source for both mechanisms. A visitor with
 * JavaScript therefore reaches our thank-you page; only a visitor without it
 * still lands on Formspree's.
 *
 * THE FIELD STAYS REGARDLESS. It is correct, it costs nothing, and it starts
 * working server-side the day the plan is upgraded — at which point the no-JS
 * visitor gets our page too. The target is not the problem: `lendavhollandlane.ee`
 * resolves and serves the site as of 26 July 2026, so this URL loads.
 *
 * If the paid plan is taken via the DASHBOARD redirect setting rather than
 * `_next`, note that setting is one URL per form and cannot vary by locale —
 * both Estonian and English visitors would land on the same page. `_next` is
 * per-submission and is why this form can send each locale to its own.
 */
const nextUrl = new URL(path('thanks', locale), site.domain).href

/** The select always has a value, so `defaultService` only chooses which. */
const selected = defaultService ?? services[0]?.data.slug
---
<form class="quote" action={formspreeAction} method="POST" aria-labelledby="quote-heading">
  <h2 id="quote-heading">{f.heading}</h2>

  <input type="hidden" name="_next" value={nextUrl} />
  <input type="hidden" name="_subject" value={f.subject} />

  {/* Honeypot. Hidden from sight, from the tab order and from assistive tech.
      A bot fills it; Formspree then discards the submission. */}
  <div class="quote__gotcha" aria-hidden="true">
    <label for="quote-gotcha">{f.gotcha}</label>
    <input id="quote-gotcha" type="text" name="_gotcha" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
  </div>

  <div class="quote__grid">
    <p class="field">
      <label for="quote-name">{f.fields.name.label}</label>
      <input id="quote-name" type="text" name="name" autocomplete="name" required />
    </p>

    <p class="field">
      <label for="quote-email">{f.fields.email.label}</label>
      <input id="quote-email" type="email" name="email" autocomplete="email"
             aria-describedby="quote-email-hint" required />
      <span class="field__hint" id="quote-email-hint">{f.fields.email.hint}</span>
    </p>

    <p class="field">
      <label for="quote-phone">
        {f.fields.phone.label} <span class="field__optional">{f.optional}</span>
      </label>
      <input id="quote-phone" type="tel" name="phone" autocomplete="tel"
             aria-describedby="quote-phone-hint" />
      <span class="field__hint" id="quote-phone-hint">{f.fields.phone.hint}</span>
    </p>

    <p class="field field--wide">
      <label for="quote-address">{f.fields.address.label}</label>
      <input id="quote-address" type="text" name="address" autocomplete="street-address"
             aria-describedby="quote-address-hint" required />
      <span class="field__hint" id="quote-address-hint">{f.fields.address.hint}</span>
    </p>

    <p class="field">
      <label for="quote-service">{f.fields.service.label}</label>
      <select id="quote-service" name="service">
        {services.map((entry) => (
          <option value={entry.data.title} selected={entry.data.slug === selected}>
            {entry.data.title}
          </option>
        ))}
      </select>
    </p>

    <p class="field">
      <label for="quote-area">
        {f.fields.area.label} <span class="field__optional">{f.optional}</span>
      </label>
      {/* `inputmode` brings up a numeric keypad without `type="number"`, whose
          spinner and locale-dependent decimal handling are wrong for "about 180". */}
      <input id="quote-area" type="text" name="area" inputmode="numeric"
             aria-describedby="quote-area-hint" />
      <span class="field__hint" id="quote-area-hint">{f.fields.area.hint}</span>
    </p>

    <p class="field field--wide">
      <label for="quote-message">
        {f.fields.message.label} <span class="field__optional">{f.optional}</span>
      </label>
      <textarea id="quote-message" name="message" rows="5"
                aria-describedby="quote-message-hint"></textarea>
      <span class="field__hint" id="quote-message-hint">{f.fields.message.hint}</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="quote__submit">
    <button class="btn btn--accent" type="submit">{f.submit}</button>
    <p class="fineprint quote__privacy">
      <strong>{f.privacyLead}</strong>{' '}
      {f.privacy.join(' ')}{' '}
      <a href={path('privacy', locale)}>{f.privacyLink}</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</form>

<style>
  .quote {
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-card);
    padding: var(--sp-7);
  }

  .quote h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-h3);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-h3);
    margin-block-end: var(--sp-6);
  }

  /* Off-screen rather than display:none — a field the browser will not render
     at all is one some bots learn to skip. Not tabbable, not announced. */
  .quote__gotcha {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .quote__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-5);
  }

  /* `align-content: start` is load-bearing, not tidying. Side-by-side fields
     are grid items of equal row height, and a field with no hint has two rows
     to fill it where its neighbour has three — so its input stretched and sat
     visibly lower than the one next to it. Packing the rows to the top makes
     labels and inputs line up across the columns whether a hint exists or not. */
  .field {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-2);
    align-content: start;
  }

  .field label {
    font-size: var(--step-sm);
    font-weight: var(--fw-strong);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .field__optional {
    font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .field__hint {
    font-size: var(--step-xs);
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .field input,
  .field select,
  .field textarea {
    width: 100%;
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-xs);
    padding: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-4);
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .field textarea {
    resize: vertical;
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
  }

  .field input:focus-visible,
  .field select:focus-visible,
  .field textarea:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
    outline-offset: 1px;
  }

  /* The browser's own "please fill in this field" bubble is the validation UI.
     This only makes an invalid field visible once the browser has judged it —
     :user-invalid, not :invalid, so an untouched required field is not red on
     first paint. */
  .field input:user-invalid,
  .field textarea:user-invalid {
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  .quote__submit {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-5);
    margin-block-start: var(--sp-7);
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .quote__privacy {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .quote__privacy strong {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .quote__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    }

    .field--wide {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }
</style>

{/* The named exception. Reasoning is in the header comment above, which Astro
    discards rather than shipping; only what is needed to read the code is kept
    inline. `is:inline` leaves the script exactly here — Astro neither bundles
    nor hoists it, so it costs no extra request.

    ONE `QuoteForm` PER PAGE TODAY (`kontakt.astro`, `en/contact.astro`). A
    second on one page would render a second copy of this script and bind both
    forms twice; add a guard then rather than carrying a dead one now. */}
<script is:inline>
  for (const form of document.querySelectorAll('form.quote')) {
    const next = form.querySelector('input[name="_next"]')?.value
    const button = form.querySelector('button[type="submit"]')
    // No `_next` to navigate to means nothing to enhance: leave the native POST alone.
    if (!next) continue
    form.addEventListener('submit', async (event) => {
      // preventDefault FIRST, then bail. Returning before it would hand the
      // event back to the browser and fire a second, native POST mid-flight.
      event.preventDefault()
      if (button?.disabled) return
      if (button) button.disabled = true
      try {
        const res = await fetch(form.action, {
          method: 'POST',
          body: new FormData(form),
          // Without this Formspree answers with the HTML redirect, not JSON.
          headers: { Accept: 'application/json' },
        })
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error('formspree ' + res.status)
        // Same-origin path, so a preview build stays on the preview build.
        location.assign(new URL(next, location.origin).pathname)
      } catch (error) {
        // A throw means the request never reached Formspree; a non-ok means
        // Formspree refused it. Neither leaves a submission on their server, so
        // re-posting natively cannot duplicate the enquiry. The exception is a
        // response lost AFTER they accepted it, which would — and that is
        // accepted: a duplicate enquiry is survivable, a lost one is not.
        console.error('quote form: AJAX submit failed, falling back to native', error)
        if (button) button.disabled = false
        form.submit()
      }
    })
  }
</script>
